  "grading_output": "**REASONING**: The candidate diagnosis is identical to the reference, matching the core lymphoma entity, non–germinal-center immunophenotype, CNS and cutaneous involvement, and secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH).\n\nThe pathology supports this diagnosis: deep skin biopsies show large atypical CD20-positive/PAX5-positive, CD3-negative cells confined to small-vessel lumina, outlined by CD31/CD34-positive endothelium, with a Ki-67 index of approximately 95%. CD10 negativity and MUM1 positivity support the stated immunophenotype. Marrow re-review also demonstrates large CD20-positive cells within sinusoids. Although the skin-biopsy order contains a contradictory negative-result parenthetical, the **actual reported result** and pathological narrative document diagnostic intravascular malignant B cells.\n\nCNS involvement is clinically supported by progressive encephalopathy and recurrent multifocal infarcts of differing ages, without a cardiac embolic source or large-vessel abnormality. Negative CSF cytology does not exclude predominantly intravascular disease. Cutaneous involvement is directly biopsy-confirmed. Lymphoma-associated secondary HLH is supported by fever, splenomegaly, elevated ferritin, hypertriglyceridemia, elevated soluble IL-2 receptor, and marrow hemophagocytosis.\n\nThere are no missing reference components, incorrect qualifiers, or unrelated diagnostic additions. This meets the rubric’s definition of a perfect match.\n\n**SCORE**: 5",
  "grading_reason": "The candidate diagnosis is identical to the reference, matching the core lymphoma entity, non–germinal-center immunophenotype, CNS and cutaneous involvement, and secondary hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH).\n\nThe pathology supports this diagnosis: deep skin biopsies show large atypical CD20-positive/PAX5-positive, CD3-negative cells confined to small-vessel lumina, outlined by CD31/CD34-positive endothelium, with a Ki-67 index of approximately 95%. CD10 negativity and MUM1 positivity support the stated immunophenotype. Marrow re-review also demonstrates large CD20-positive cells within sinusoids. Although the skin-biopsy order contains a contradictory negative-result parenthetical, the **actual reported result** and pathological narrative document diagnostic intravascular malignant B cells.\n\nCNS involvement is clinically supported by progressive encephalopathy and recurrent multifocal infarcts of differing ages, without a cardiac embolic source or large-vessel abnormality. Negative CSF cytology does not exclude predominantly intravascular disease. Cutaneous involvement is directly biopsy-confirmed. Lymphoma-associated secondary HLH is supported by fever, splenomegaly, elevated ferritin, hypertriglyceridemia, elevated soluble IL-2 receptor, and marrow hemophagocytosis.\n\nThere are no missing reference components, incorrect qualifiers, or unrelated diagnostic additions. This meets the rubric’s definition of a perfect match."
